  "subjective_assessment": {
    "conclusion": "The accepted renderer is visibly calmer and more cohesive: Moss is the focal character, actions read as anticipation/contact/settle rather than generic slides, manipulated objects stay physically bound, and the room reads as a single authored storybook diorama.",
    "method": "Human/visual inspection of the real 800x480 Canvas; no automated beauty score.",
    "weaknesses_ranked": [
      "Live canonical updates could rebase animation from the prior semantic endpoint and cause a visible teleport during an in-flight transition.",
      "Most actions shared a generic timing/interpolation language, so valid behavior still looked procedural instead of staged.",
      "Moss had a weak icon-like silhouette and insufficiently distinct carry/sleep/contact poses.",
      "The room mixed material/contrast languages and lacked enough contact/depth cues to read as one physical diorama.",
      "Ambient rain/dust and idle motion competed too strongly with Moss.",
      "Action-family alternation allowed valid probabilistic behavior to read as visual indecision/ping-pong."
    ]
  },
